const terms=[
{term:'Picardía',kind:'A character name and an ordinary Spanish noun',meaning:'Cunning, slyness, resourcefulness, or mischievous cleverness. Depending on context, the word can suggest playful wit or dishonest tricks.',context:'Cruz’s son is known as Picardía. His story includes card games, deception, hardship and an effort to change his reputation. In Part Two, Canto XXVI, he says that even after correcting his faults he could not escape the name. It is therefore a meaningful nickname, not proof that every act he performs is deceitful.',read:'/en/students/vuelta-canto-26/'},
{term:'Gaucho',kind:'Cultural and historical term',meaning:'A rural horseman associated with the grasslands of Argentina and neighboring regions. Not simply an American cowboy.',context:'The term describes work, skills, social position and a contested literary identity. Fierro speaks as one particular gaucho, not as every rural worker.',read:'/en/students/canto-2/'},
{term:'Payada',kind:'Oral poetry and music',meaning:'A traditional poetic performance in which singers improvise or exchange sung verses, often accompanied by a guitar.',context:'The Moreno and Fierro meet in a contest of ideas and verse. The performance is more than entertainment: it revisits unresolved violence.',read:'/en/students/vuelta-canto-30/'},
{term:'Payador',kind:'Person',meaning:'A singer-poet who performs a payada.',context:'A payador’s skill lies in verbal invention, musical performance and answering challenges.',read:'/en/students/vuelta-canto-30/'},
{term:'Pulpería',kind:'Rural institution',meaning:'A countryside store and meeting place where people could buy provisions, talk, drink and sometimes gather for music.',context:'The pulpería is a social setting, not merely a modern bar. Some of the poem’s confrontations take place in these shared spaces.',read:'/en/students/canto-7/'},
{term:'Fortín',kind:'Frontier institution',meaning:'A small military outpost on the nineteenth-century frontier.',context:'Fierro describes harsh conditions and exploitation during service. The poem’s account should be read beside independent frontier history.',read:'/en/students/canto-4/'},
{term:'Leva',kind:'Military and political term',meaning:'Compulsory recruitment or the seizure of men for military service.',context:'It helps explain why Fierro’s forced frontier service destroys his previous family life.',read:'/en/students/canto-3/'},
{term:'Partida',kind:'Armed group',meaning:'A patrol or group assembled for a particular task; in this poem, often a force pursuing someone.',context:'Cruz belongs to the group that hunts Fierro before he chooses to defend him.',read:'/en/students/canto-9/'},
{term:'Facón',kind:'Material culture',meaning:'A long knife commonly associated with the historical gaucho.',context:'Descriptions of knife fighting must not be confused with an endorsement of the violence.',read:'/en/students/canto-7/'},
{term:'Mate',kind:'Drink and material culture',meaning:'An infusion made from yerba mate leaves; the name can also mean the vessel used to drink it.',context:'A shared daily practice in rural and urban Rioplatense life, with meanings that vary by setting.',read:'/en/students/canto-2/'},
{term:'Paisano',kind:'Social address',meaning:'A countryman, rural fellow, or person from the same land or community, depending on context.',context:'In the poem it can mark solidarity and shared rural experience without implying that every person shares Fierro’s opinions.',read:'/en/students/vuelta-canto-33/'},
{term:'Moreno',kind:'Historically situated descriptor',meaning:'A term referring to dark complexion; in the poem it designates an Afro-descendant singer and also appears in the story of an earlier victim.',context:'The Moreno of the later payada is not the man killed in the first part. Translate and discuss the label carefully in its nineteenth-century context.',read:'/en/students/vuelta-canto-29/'},
{term:'Viejo Vizcacha',kind:'Character name and animal reference',meaning:'Viejo means “old”; vizcacha is the Spanish name of a South American burrowing rodent.',context:'The character’s nickname invites interpretive comparisons with the animal, but a literary reading should separate established meaning from speculation about the author’s intent.',read:'/en/students/vuelta-canto-15/'},
{term:'Canto',kind:'Literary structure',meaning:'A numbered section or song of a longer poem; roughly a chapter, but organized as poetry.',context:'The two parts of Martín Fierro contain 13 and 33 cantos respectively.',read:'/en/students/'},
{term:'Estrofa',kind:'Literary structure',meaning:'A stanza: a group of poetic lines or verses.',context:'The original Spanish corpus preserves numbered stanzas so readers can locate each passage precisely.',read:'/en/students/'},
{term:'Verso',kind:'Literary structure',meaning:'A line of poetry, not necessarily a complete sentence.',context:'The site preserves the poem’s original lines and permanent references.',read:'/en/students/'}
