<Base lang="en-US" pageType="CollectionPage" title="Martín Fierro Glossary for Students: Gaucho Words Explained" description="A contextual English glossary of gaucho vocabulary, rural institutions, poetry and character names in Martín Fierro, including the meaning of Picardía.">
<main class="glossary"><nav class="crumb"><a href="/en/">English edition</a> / <a href="/en/students/">For students</a> / Glossary</nav><header><p class="eyebrow">FOR STUDENTS · WORDS IN CONTEXT</p><h1>Gaucho words and names, explained</h1><p class="deck">A practical English companion to José Hernández’s <em>Martín Fierro</em>. These terms are best understood through the story, not by replacing every Spanish word with a single English equivalent.</p></header>
<section class="spotlight"><h2>Why is Cruz’s son called “Picardía”?</h2><p><em>Picardía</em> is an ordinary Spanish noun meaning cunning, craftiness, mischievous cleverness or sly resourcefulness. As a nickname, it draws attention to the young man’s experiences and reputation. Yet the poem makes a more complicated point: he describes trying to correct his faults while finding that the name still follows him. A name can shape how others judge someone even after that person changes.</p><p><a href="#picardia">Read the full glossary entry</a> · <a href="/en/students/vuelta-canto-26/">Study Part Two, Canto 26</a> · <a href="/en/students/characters/#picardia">Meet the character</a></p></section>
