<div class="entries">{terms.map(t=><section class="entry" id={t.term.toLowerCase().normalize('NFD').replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g,'').replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,'-')}><p class="eyebrow">{t.kind}</p><h2>{t.term}</h2><p><strong>Meaning in English:</strong> {t.meaning}</p><p><strong>In Martín Fierro:</strong> {t.context}</p><p><a href={t.read}>Explore the related canto guide</a></p></section>)}</div>
<section class="method"><h2>How we explain historical language</h2><p>Definitions are explanations for readers, not replacements for Hernández’s Spanish verses. Some spellings and expressions in the poem reflect the gaucho literary voice, rural oral traditions and nineteenth-century usage. A word’s historical or literary meaning may differ from its everyday modern use. Interpretations are distinguished from documented events.</p><p><strong>References:</strong> José Hernández, <em>El gaucho Martín Fierro</em> (1872) and <em>La vuelta de Martín Fierro</em> (1879), in this site’s structured original corpus; <a href="https://dictionary.cambridge.org/es/diccionario/espanol-ingles/picardia">Cambridge Spanish–English Dictionary: picardía</a>; <a href="https://www.asale.org/damer/viveza">ASALE Dictionary of Americanisms: viveza criolla</a>. This is a selective student glossary, not an exhaustive historical dictionary.</p></section></main>
